": -11.0, "environment": 7.38, "incumbency": 0.0, "money": -0.13}, "rating_margin": -13.9, "sigma_race": 6.0, "sigma_total": 6.61, "baseline_margin": -9.31, "analyst_adjustment": 0.31, "key_factors": ["Louisiana's strong Republican lean (R+11) in an open-seat race", "Julia Letlow's large fundraising and outside-spending advantage", "Prediction markets price Democratic chances at only 3-10%", "Most expert ratings call the race Solid or Safe Republican"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a Republican hold: the state leans R+11, Letlow has out-raised Davis and benefits from heavy outside spending, and markets plus most raters see little doubt. The two Democratic-sponsored polls showing a 4-5 point race temper the outlook slightly, but their partisan sponsorship and thin sample argue against moving far from the consensus.", "watch": "Any independent polling confirming the tight numbers from the Hart survey, or a surge in Democratic outside spending, would narrow the expected margin.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Bill Cassidy (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Louisiana's open Senate seat favors Julia Letlow, who has raised nearly $7 million and drawn more than $11 million in outside support, against Democrat Jamie Davis's $4 million and a grassroots campaign. The state's Republican lean and the absence of incumbent Bill Cassidy from the ballot still leave the seat in Republican hands on paper.\n\nTwo Democratic-sponsored polls show a surprisingly close 4-5 point race, and Davis has been on the air with ads targeting Letlow. But prediction markets give Davis only a 3-10 percent chance, and most expert ratings call the race Solid or Safe Republican. The forecast expects Letlow to win by roughly nine points, with meaningful uncertainty given the thin and partisan polling.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0491, "margin": -9.91}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0664, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0312, "